{# ═══ CHAPTER 1 ═══ #}
<div class="book-chapter">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-num">Chapter One</div>
        <h2 class="chapter-title">The Wealth Architect vs. The Nomad</h2>
    </div>
    <img src="/assets/book/ch1-wealth-architect.png" alt="Wealth Architect lifestyle" class="chapter-img">

    <p>There are two types of remote workers in 2026. The first is the <strong>Digital Nomad</strong> — someone who picks a city because the Instagram photos look nice, the rent is cheap, and the coworking space has decent WiFi. The second is the <strong>Wealth Architect</strong> — someone who picks a city because the tax treaty saves them $14,000 a year, the visa leads to permanent residency in five years, and the cost of living means they bank 60% of their after-tax income.</p>

    <p>Both people might end up in Lisbon. But one is on holiday. The other is building generational wealth.</p>

    <h3>The Mindset Shift</h3>
    <p>A Wealth Architect treats their location like a portfolio allocation. Every country has a <strong>Total Cost of Residency (TCR)</strong> — the sum of your tax liability, cost of living, visa fees, healthcare, and opportunity cost. Most nomads only look at rent. That is like buying a stock based on its logo.</p>

    <p>Your salary is not what your employer pays you. <strong>Your salary is what you keep after the government, the landlord, and the healthcare provider take their cut.</strong> The Wealth Architect's entire strategy is to minimise those three numbers simultaneously.</p>

    <div class="book-callout emerald">
        <div class="callout-title">KEY PRINCIPLE</div>
        <p><strong>Net Disposable Income = Gross Income − Tax Liability − Cost of Living − Visa/Legal Fees.</strong> Optimise all four variables. Never optimise just one.</p>
    </div>

    <p>This book will teach you to think in TCR. By the time you finish Chapter 8, you will know exactly which city on Earth makes you the richest version of yourself in 2026 — backed by real data, not blog posts.</p>
</div>

{# ═══ CHAPTER 2 ═══ #}
<div class="book-chapter">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-num">Chapter Two</div>
        <h2 class="chapter-title">The "Habit Tax" &amp; The Cheap Rent Trap</h2>
    </div>

    <p>Every year, thousands of remote workers move to Bali, Medellín, or Chiang Mai because rent is $600–$800 per month. They post a celebratory tweet. Then, 12 months later, they realise they paid <strong>30% of their gross income</strong> in taxes to a country they no longer live in — and another 15% in self-employment tax on top.</p>

    <p>This is the <strong>Cheap Rent Trap</strong>. It goes like this:</p>

    <div class="book-callout">
        <div class="callout-title">THE TRAP IN NUMBERS</div>
        <p>You earn <strong>$120,000/year</strong>. You move to Bali. Rent is $800/mo ($9,600/year). Amazing, right?</p>
        <p>But you are still a US tax resident. Federal + SE tax = <strong>~$33,600/year</strong>.</p>
        <p>Your "savings" from cheap rent: <strong>$14,400</strong> vs. a US apartment at $2,000/mo.</p>
        <p>Your tax bill you ignored: <strong>$33,600</strong>.</p>
        <p><strong>You saved $14,400 on rent but lost $33,600 to taxes.</strong> Net loss: $19,200.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The Wealth Architect does not move for cheap rent. They move for <strong>cheap taxes</strong>. A country with $1,200 rent but a 7% flat tax saves you more than a country with $600 rent and a 35% marginal rate.</p>

    <h3>The "Habit Tax"</h3>
    <p>There is a second trap that nobody talks about: the <strong>Habit Tax</strong>. When you move to a cheaper country, your spending habits do not magically reset. You still order takeaway four nights a week. You still subscribe to six streaming services. You still buy premium coffee every morning.</p>

    <p>Research from the 2025 Nomad Finance Survey found that remote workers who relocated to "cheap" countries reduced their spending by only <strong>12% on average</strong> — despite cost-of-living indices suggesting a 40–60% reduction. The gap? Habit Tax.</p>

    <table class="book-table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Annual Rent</th><th>Tax Bill</th><th>Net Cost</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bali (no tax planning)</td><td>$9,600</td><td>$33,600</td><td class="warning">$43,200</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Dubai (0% income tax)</td><td>$24,000</td><td>$0</td><td class="highlight">$24,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Spain (Beckham Law)</td><td>$14,400</td><td>$12,000</td><td class="highlight">$26,400</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p><strong>The lesson:</strong> Never separate rent from tax. Your Total Cost of Residency is the only number that matters.</p>
</div>

{# ═══ CHAPTER 3 ═══ #}
<div class="book-chapter">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-num">Chapter Three</div>
        <h2 class="chapter-title">The 2026 Global Tax Matrix</h2>
    </div>
    <img src="/assets/book/ch3-tax-matrix.png" alt="Global Tax Matrix" class="chapter-img">

    <p>This is the chapter you will bookmark. These are the <strong>Tier 1 tax jurisdictions</strong> for high-earning remote workers in 2026 — each offering a legal mechanism to dramatically reduce your effective tax rate.</p>

    <h3>🇪🇸 Spain — The Beckham Law</h3>
    <p>Spain's <em>Régimen Especial de Impatriados</em> (commonly called the "Beckham Law") allows new tax residents to pay a <strong>flat 24% rate</strong> on Spanish-sourced income up to €600,000 — instead of the standard progressive rates that peak at 47%. You qualify if you haven't been a Spanish tax resident in the previous 5 years.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Freelancers and employees earning €60K–€200K who want EU residency with a predictable tax bill.</p>

    <h3>🇮🇹 Italy — Residenza Digitale</h3>
    <p>Italy's 2024 Digital Nomad Visa evolved into the <strong>Residenza Digitale</strong> framework in 2025. Combined with the <em>Regime Forfettario</em> for qualifying freelancers (revenue under €85,000), your effective tax rate drops to approximately <strong>5%</strong> on income. For higher earners, the <em>Impatriati</em> regime offers a 70% income exemption.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Solo freelancers under €85K who want dolce vita at a 5% rate.</p>

    <h3>🇦🇪 UAE — The Zero-Tax Engine</h3>
    <p>The UAE charges <strong>0% personal income tax</strong>. Corporate tax (introduced 2023) is 9% on profits above AED 375,000, but freelancers operating under a Free Zone licence with no UAE-sourced clients pay effectively zero. The Dubai Freelance Permit costs approximately <strong>$1,800/year</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Anyone earning $100K+ who can handle the heat and wants absolute tax freedom.</p>

    <h3>🇵🇹 Portugal — NHR 2.0</h3>
    <p>Portugal's Non-Habitual Resident programme was restructured in 2024. The new "NHR 2.0" (<em>Incentivo Fiscal à Investigação Científica</em>) now targets researchers and tech workers with a <strong>20% flat rate</strong> on qualifying employment income. Foreign-sourced passive income may still be exempt under certain treaty provisions.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Tech professionals who qualify under the scientific/innovation categories.</p>

    <h3>🇬🇷 Greece — The 50% Exemption</h3>
    <p>Greece offers a <strong>50% income tax exemption for 7 years</strong> to new tax residents who transfer their employment to Greece. Effective top rate drops from 44% to approximately 22%. Combined with low cost of living in Athens or Thessaloniki, this is an emerging powerhouse.</p>

    <table class="book-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Programme</th><th>Effective Rate</th><th>Duration</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>🇪🇸 Spain</td><td>Beckham Law</td><td class="highlight">24% flat</td><td>6 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>🇮🇹 Italy</td><td>Forfettario</td><td class="highlight">~5%</td><td>5 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td>🇦🇪 UAE</td><td>Free Zone</td><td class="highlight">0%</td><td>Indefinite</td></tr>
